I had no framework to go on, so I assumed React with TypeScript. The core logic is a pure function you can port elsewhere.

```tsx
// Pagination.tsx
import React from "react";

type PageItem = number | "ellipsis-start" | "ellipsis-end";

/** Returns the list of items to render. With one page this is just [1]. */
export function getPageItems(
  currentPage: number,
  totalPages: number,
  siblings = 1
): PageItem[] {
  const total = Math.max(1, Math.floor(totalPages));
  const current = Math.min(Math.max(1, currentPage), total);

  // Show everything if it fits: first + last + current + siblings + 2 ellipses
  const maxWithoutEllipsis = siblings * 2 + 5;
  if (total <= maxWithoutEllipsis) {
    return Array.from({ length: total }, (_, i) => i + 1); // total=1 -> [1]
  }

  const left = Math.max(current - siblings, 2);
  const right = Math.min(current + siblings, total - 1);
  const items: PageItem[] = [1];

  if (left > 2) items.push("ellipsis-start");
  for (let p = left; p <= right; p++) items.push(p);
  if (right < total - 1) items.push("ellipsis-end");

  items.push(total);
  return items;
}

interface PaginationProps {
  totalItems: number;
  pageSize: number;
  currentPage: number;
  onPageChange: (page: number) => void;
}

export function Pagination({
  totalItems,
  pageSize,
  currentPage,
  onPageChange,
}: PaginationProps) {
  // Zero results still counts as one (empty) page, so "1" is always shown.
  const totalPages = Math.max(1, Math.ceil(totalItems / Math.max(1, pageSize)));
  const current = Math.min(Math.max(1, currentPage), totalPages);
  const items = getPageItems(current, totalPages);

  return (
    <nav aria-label="Pagination">
      <ul style={{ display: "flex", gap: 4, listStyle: "none", padding: 0 }}>
        <li>
          <button
            onClick={() => onPageChange(current - 1)}
            disabled={current <= 1}
            aria-label="Previous page"
          >
            ‹
          </button>
        </li>

        {items.map((item) =>
          typeof item === "number" ? (
            <li key={item}>
              <button
                onClick={() => onPageChange(item)}
                aria-current={item === current ? "page" : undefined}
                disabled={item === current}
              >
                {item}
              </button>
            </li>
          ) : (
            <li key={item} aria-hidden="true">…</li>
          )
        )}

        <li>
          <button
            onClick={() => onPageChange(current + 1)}
            disabled={current >= totalPages}
            aria-label="Next page"
          >
            ›
          </button>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </nav>
  );
}
```

Behavior with exactly one page (for example `totalItems=8, pageSize=10`):
- `totalPages` is 1, so `getPageItems` returns `[1]` and the component renders a single page button, "1".
- That button is marked `aria-current="page"` and disabled.
- Previous and Next are both disabled, since `current <= 1` and `current >= totalPages` are both true.
- Zero results also gives one page, and an out-of-range `currentPage` is clamped to 1.

